{"id":"the-complete-alice-in-wonderland-en_sct106_f1","schemaVersion":1,"fragmentId":"sha1:18e7d839d2d63ab8bea39a7a0897606e5bf2b3e5","generationHash":"sha1:2ce838f72dd8f7e4a44313aa3b8aeacb4b620d53","title":"The Complete Alice in Wonderland - The Last Letter to Alice (Fragment 1)","text":"The Last Letter to Alice\n\nCHRIST CHURCH, Oxford\n\nDecember 8, 1891\n\nMy Dear Mrs. Hargreaves,\n\nI should be so glad if you could, quite conveniently to yourself, look in for tea any day. You would probably prefer to bring a companion: but I must leave the choice to you, only remarking that if your husband is here he would be (most) very welcome. (I crossed out “most” because it’s ambiguous; most words are, I fear.) I met him in our Common Room not long ago. It was hard to realise that he was the husband of one I can scarcely picture to myself, even now, as more than 7 years old!\n\nAlways sincerely yours,\n\nC. L. Dodgson\n\n(P.S.) Your adventures have had a marvellous success. I have now sold well over 100,000 copies.\n\n(Sadly, Alice was not able to meet with Lewis Carroll for tea this one last time. But she did visit the elderly gentleman, along with her sister Rhoda. What they spoke of will never be known. It was the last time they ever met.)","meta":{"bookId":"the-complete-alice-in-wonderland-en","bookTitle":"The Complete Alice in Wonderland","bookLanguage":"en","author":"Lewis Carroll","seriesId":"alice","seriesTitle":"Alice","sectionIndex":106,"sectionTitle":"The Last Letter to Alice","fragmentIndex":1,"fragmentsInSection":1,"sourceFile":"chapter_106.xhtml","stats":{"wordCount":177,"uniqueWordCount":135,"paragraphCount":9,"vocabularyCount":53,"levelDistribution":{"A1":14,"A2":10,"B1":12,"B2":12,"C1":4,"C2":1}}},"vocabulary":[{"word":"able","lemma":"able","lemmaId":"able","pos":"adjective","source":"word","wordKey":"able_adjective","level":"A2","translations":{"ru":{"translation":"способный"},"uk":{"translation":"здатних"}},"_spacyLemma":"able","example":"(Sadly, Alice wa